statement:
Each box is one unit wide, and `boxes[i]` is the height of box `i`. A warehouse is a row of `warehouse.length` rooms, each one unit wide, where room `j` (counted from the left, starting at 0) has height `warehouse[j]`.

Boxes are pushed in one at a time, and each box may enter through **either** end of the warehouse, moving inward from that side:

- a box can enter a room only when the room's height is at least the box's height, so a lower room blocks it;
- boxes are never stacked, so each room holds at most one box, and a box cannot move past a room that already holds one;
- you may choose any subset of the boxes, push them in any order and from either side, and leave each box in any room it can reach.

Return the largest number of boxes that can end up inside the warehouse.

validator:
function validate([boxes, warehouse]) {
  if (!Array.isArray(boxes) || boxes.length < 1 || boxes.length > 100000) return 'boxes needs 1 to 10^5 heights.';
  if (!Array.isArray(warehouse) || warehouse.length < 1 || warehouse.length > 100000) return 'warehouse needs 1 to 10^5 rooms.';
  if (boxes.some(h => !Number.isInteger(h) || h < 1 || h > 1e9)) return 'Each box height is an integer from 1 to 10^9.';
  if (warehouse.some(h => !Number.isInteger(h) || h < 1 || h > 1e9)) return 'Each room height is an integer from 1 to 10^9.';
  return null;
}

